item.content:
Adjunctive therapy with antidepressants for the treatment of major depressive disorder (MDD) in adults   [see 
                                 Clinical Studies (


                                 14.3


                                 )
                              ].

text.paragraph:
The recommended CAPLYTA dosage in patients who receive [see 
                                 Drug Interactions (


                                 7.1


                                 )
                              ]:

CAPLYTA is not approved for the treatment of patients with dementia-related psychosis [see 
                                 Indications and Usage (


                                 1


                                 )
                              ].

tr.td:
Concomitant use of CAPLYTA with CYP3A4 inducers decreases the exposure of lumateperone [see Clinical Pharmacology (
12.3
) ].

Reduce the CAPLYTA dosage when used concomitantly with moderate or strong CYP3A4 inhibitors [see Dosage and Administration (
2.2
) ].

Concomitant use of CAPLYTA with moderate or strong CYP3A4 inhibitors increases lumateperone exposure [see Clinical Pharmacology (
12.3
)], which may increase the risk of adverse reactions.

text.paragraph:
Patients with moderate hepatic impairment (HI) (Child-Pugh class B) and severe HI (Child-Pugh class C) generally had higher exposure to lumateperone than patients with normal hepatic function; therefore, the recommended CAPLYTA dosage in patients with moderate or severe HI is lower than those with normal hepatic function [see 
                                 Dosage and Administration (


                                 2.3


                                 )
                               and 
                                 Clinical Pharmacology (


                                 12.3


                                 )
                              ].
